sri lanka's political crisis is now  hitting citizens at the gas pump with  record high prices  in an effort to conserve fuel the  embattled government is asking people to  work from home and has close schools  restaurants have switched to cooking  meals with wood thousands of protesters  enraged by the country's worsening  economic crisis stormed the presidential  compound and the prime minister's  private home over the weekend both the  president and prime minister have agreed  to step down opposition leaders are  racing to form a new government  we take a closer look now at how the  crisis is affecting the lives of  citizens in the country's capital  colombo  for seven days  has been waiting here in a kilometer  long line for gas in sri lanka's capital  colombo  he'd heard a new fuel delivery was  coming he spent day and night here since  there is simply no more fuel  we have no more gas at home to cook  we used to have an income but for weeks  now the money has stopped coming  we can't go on like this  life is hard  to make matters worse his wife is ill  he tries to spend a little time with her  every day at lunchtime  risking losing his place in line  i keep calling him when he is there  waiting it's the tormenting thoughts  that have made me sick  i was even admitted to hospital  we didn't even have fuel for him to  drive me there  many people in sri lanka face similar  problems inflation is soaring and prices  are skyrocketing even for essentials  like food  hardly anyone can afford to buy what  they need  people used to have three meals a day  most of the time  now it's either two or one  sometimes people are drinking water  instead of eating  we don't know what to do what to give  our children they say they're hungry but  we don't have anything for them  we can't drink tea with milk anymore we  have no milk powder  we just about managed to buy rice but  even that is very expensive  another day has gone by in the line for  gas  and for azam  it's again been fruitless  his one hope that after the resignation  of president gotabaya rajapaksa better  times will finally come  only when we get problems like the fuel  crisis solved can we live a life worth  living  i haven't lost faith yet that a new  government will give us fresh hope and  change will finally happen  until then azam muhaddin will spend  another night in line hoping that he  will finally get a few litres of fuel  lawyer and human rights campaigner  pavani fonseca joins us from the center  for policy alternatives in colombo there  are reports that the president and his  brother are already attempting to leave  the country uh can you tell us how this  is going down with protesters  well what we've seen in the last few  days is quite remarkable in terms of the  numbers that have arrived at the protest  sites and as a result  we've seen the president agreeing to  resign as well as the prime minister  agreeing to resign they have yet to  resign and this is something we need to  keep in mind that a few days later  we still have a president and prime  minister holding on to office and now  it's been reported that the president  and his younger brother attempted to  leave sri lanka  we are waiting for verified information  but it also looks like people  at the airport as well as staff members  prevented them from leaving so there's  real anger and frustration among people  in sri lanka and it looks like people  also want  a reckoning from the political  leadership for the prison crisis  um i i want to bring us back to this  storming of the presidential palace the  the  footage we saw was absolutely amazing  was it a was it a clear sign  that that this is the end of president  rachael and his family's grip on  politics  well we need to keep in mind that the  protests started several months ago the  protest started in march and it's been  expanded and spread to different paths  of sri lanka and the demand has been  very much clearly for the resignation of  the president as well as the departure  of the rajapaksas from active politics  so the warning signs have been there  people have been peacefully protesting  for months what happened on saturday as  you quite rightly said was remarkable in  terms of the numbers that came out and  the fact that after months of protesting  they were able to breach and enter both  the presidential palace as well as the  official office the secretariat  so that sent a clear message that time  was up and the president had to resign  as well as the prime minister and in  that context it's still shocking that  they're still holding on to office  because the people have been very clear  with their demand  when it comes to the process of  appointing new political leaders  will they have the legitimacy to bring  about the political stability that's  needed in the country  well we are in a political and economic  crisis and stability is critical and  it's incumbent on anyone who's going to  take on the job that this is going to be  given priority to ensure that there is  economic recovery now it's going to be a  mammoth task as to whoever takes it on  how they're going to address this and it  will require a lot of attention and  support  but there's a clear path in terms of a  resignation of the president the  constitution provides for a clear path  there can be an acting prime minister  president who can be in office for 30  days and it's up to parliament to then  elect a member of parliament to serve  the remainder of the period so there's a  clear constitutional provision and a  pathway to address that whoever takes it  on  will also require the support of  parliament so their legitimacy will  really be dependent and will be tested  in the coming weeks and months as to  whether they are able to address it the  the opposition opposition leader has  come forward and said he he is  interested so the next few days are  going to be interesting and and fluid in  sri lanka palani fonseca thank you very  much for bringing us up to date on the  situation there
 

